e last thing I'd like to point out  about this theory is that it's fully  deterministic in other words if I know a  particle started out in exactly this  position with this speed then I can use  bohmian mechanics to calculate exactly  where we'll be at any time in the future  just like with Newton's laws there is no  randomness in it but how can that work  we know that there's randomness in the  results of quantum experiments you can  do the same experiment twice and get  different results  how can pilot wave theory explain this  if it's deterministic the answer is that  that randomness is not true randomness  think of a coin it seems like if you  flip a coin then the result is perfectly  random there's no way that you can  figure out where there will be heads or  tails but that's not actually true if  you know which side the coin started on  and all the forces that affected it in  theory you can use Newton's laws to  figure out exactly what you'll get so it  only looks random because we don't know  all the forces I'm going to call this  type of randomness randomness because of  ignorance pilot-wave theory proposes  that the randomness we see in these  experiments is of this type on the other  hand notice how according to quantum  mechanics there is no extra information  that you can gather or calculations that  you can do that will tell you exactly  what the result will be quantum  mechanics has true randomness okay so  how does the theory actually work  remember that Newton's laws tell us that  if you have a particle you can figure  out where it's going to go by knowing  all the forces pushing it some of the  forces that were used to our gravity and  electromagnetism  well biomechanics is just Newton's laws  with one slight adjustment there's an  extra force we'll call this force the  quantum force and this is the really  important bit you figure out what  strength and direction that force pulls  a certain particle using the wave  function you use the exact same wave  function for quantum mechanics for that  particle to calculate the quantum force  I won't explain how that calculation is  done but the point is that the wave  function still plays a central role in  our new theory it's just not the same  kind of role as in quantum mechanics  here the wave function sort of guides  the particle on its path that's why the  theory gets called pilot wave theory  well now we have this extra force that  pulls and pushes a particle so that the  particle will move in some kind of path  how can we use this to explain all of  our quantum experiments let me  illustrate with an impressive example  the double slit experiment hopefully you  remember it we shoot particles through  and they get through if they go through  either one or both of these holes and  then land on a screen where they're  detected the result of course is the  interference pattern  let's try get this result with bohmian  mechanics we assume each particle starts  off somewhere around here and they all  move forward and a few of them make it  to the slits say a particular particle  got to this point inside the slits we  can use the wave function to calculate  its path and it looks pretty crazy  notice if there was no quantum force  there wouldn't be any other forces on it  at all so Newton's laws would tell us  that it would just keep going at the  speed it's going which would look more  like this now what if instead of  starting at that point we started it  very very nearby you see that the  particle now goes on a completely  different path and if you start  somewhere different again it goes  somewhere else but when we originally  shot our particles we weren't 100% sure  exactly where they were so you might  shoot two particles from what you think  is the same spot but according to  bohmian mechanics they could end up in  very different places making the results  look random but the theory says that  this randomness is just randomness from  ignorance ignorance in this case of the  starting positions and it's no more  mysterious than a coin flip now we can  repeat the same calculations for many  particles getting through the slits as  you can see an interference pattern has  emerged how did this work out so  perfectly it all comes back to that  quantum force it's based off the wave  function and designed specifically so  that of the particle is moving under its  influence most will end up where the  wave function is big  basically that force is rigged to make  it give the same answer as quantum  mechanics this leads to some weird  conclusions though imagine we cover up  one slit now let's calculate where a  particle should end  up here's one particles path now we  might naively expect that if we open up  the second slit its path will still look  the same  that's not the case though what changed  the wavefunction the particle itself  doesn't go through both slits but the  wave function does therefore the force  on the particle is now different so in a  way this superposition and interference  business hasn't completely gone away  it's just more hidden now well that's  all I'm going to say about how the  theory works but I'll finish with some  quick thoughts I think pilot-wave theory  is very impressive in the way that it  gives the same answers as quantum but to  me it's most impressive in that it's  shown two completely different  explanations about reality can both  explain the facts will we someday be  able to test which one is true if so how  and if not what does that say about  physics are we really finding out the  truth or are we just telling stories  that work
 

